From 9dd6d6d3fbe564134548fb191ba7ba9a5a77edd8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Eduardo=20Garc=C3=ADa-M=C3=A1dico=20Portabella?=
 <woodyst@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 23:09:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 101/106] ASoC: qcom: sm8250: no esperar al propio trabajo al
 cancelar el plazo

El camino de exito de probe() cancelaba el temporizador del plazo con
cancel_delayed_work_sync(). Pero probe() puede estar ejecutandose DENTRO
de ese mismo elemento de trabajo: sm8250_bt_deadline_expired() llama a
device_reprobe(), que reentra en sm8250_platform_probe() de forma
sincrona en el mismo hilo de la cola de trabajo. La variante _sync espera
a que el trabajo en curso termine, es decir, se espera a si misma.

La tarjeta llegaba a registrarse (el bloqueo ocurre despues), asi que la
prueba parecia buena; 120 s mas tarde el detector de tareas colgadas
mataba el movil, que arranca con hung_task_panic=1:

  Kernel panic - not syncing: hung_task: blocked tasks
    sm8250_platform_probe+0x22c/0x28c [snd_soc_sm8250]
    really_probe / driver_probe_device / __device_attach
    device_reprobe+0x6c/0xa4
    sm8250_bt_deadline_expired+0x54/0x280 [snd_soc_sm8250]
    process_scheduled_works

La cancelacion no bloqueante basta: bt_probe_settled, puesto justo antes,
ya convierte en no-op a un temporizador en curso o posterior, y el
temporizador es de un solo disparo (sm8250_bt_deadline_check() solo lo
arma en el primer aplazamiento).

Verificado en surya: plazo cumplido, tarjeta registrada, y 195 s despues
sigue vivo sin kworkers bloqueados ni hung_task.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sound/soc/qcom/sm8250.c | 30 ++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sound/soc/qcom/sm8250.c b/sound/soc/qcom/sm8250.c
index 33fce3997..232415c51 100644
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/sm8250.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/sm8250.c
@@ -302,7 +302,7 @@ static void sm8250_bt_deadline_expired(struct work_struct *work);
 /*
  * Statically initialized on purpose, not via INIT_DELAYED_WORK() inside
  * probe(): probe() only reaches that branch when Bluetooth is actually
- * deferred, but the unconditional cancel_delayed_work_sync() on the
+ * deferred, but the unconditional cancel_delayed_work() on the
  * ordinary success path (below) has to be safe to call every single time,
  * including the common case where this work item was never armed at all.
  * Cancelling a work item that was declared like this but never scheduled is
@@ -369,10 +369,11 @@ static void sm8250_disable_dai_link_node(struct device_node *parent, const char
  * Guarded by bt_probe_settled: if the card already registered through an
  * ordinary retry before this fires (Bluetooth turned up in time after all),
  * calling device_reprobe() here would tear down a working card just to
- * probe it again. bt_deadline_work is also cancelled synchronously the
- * moment probe succeeds (see below), so reaching this function at all
- * without the flag set should not happen in practice; the check is a second
- * line of defence, not the only one.
+ * probe it again. bt_deadline_work is also cancelled the moment probe
+ * succeeds (see below - with the non-waiting cancel_delayed_work(), which
+ * matters because probe() can be running inside this very work item), so
+ * reaching this function at all without the flag set should not happen in
+ * practice; the check is a second line of defence, not the only one.
  */
 static void sm8250_bt_deadline_expired(struct work_struct *work)
 {
@@ -503,7 +504,24 @@ static int sm8250_platform_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	mutex_lock(&bt_deadline_lock);
 	bt_probe_settled = true;
 	mutex_unlock(&bt_deadline_lock);
-	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&bt_deadline_work);
+
+	/*
+	 * NOT the _sync variant. cancel_delayed_work_sync() waits for a running
+	 * work item to finish, and this probe() can BE that work item:
+	 * sm8250_bt_deadline_expired() calls device_reprobe(), which re-enters
+	 * probe() synchronously on the same workqueue thread. Waiting there is
+	 * a deadlock against ourselves - the card registers fine, then the
+	 * kworker never returns and hung_task_panic reboots the machine two
+	 * minutes later. (Seen on surya: hung_task panic with
+	 * sm8250_platform_probe <- device_reprobe <- sm8250_bt_deadline_expired
+	 * on the stack.)
+	 *
+	 * The non-waiting cancel is sufficient: bt_probe_settled, set just
+	 * above, already makes a timer that is running or fires later a no-op,
+	 * and the timer is one-shot - sm8250_bt_deadline_check() only arms it
+	 * on the first defer.
+	 */
+	cancel_delayed_work(&bt_deadline_work);
 
 	return 0;
 }
